Source: Android common kernel / Samsung S5E9945 vendor tree, kernel/user_namespace.c
Vendor tree: 6.1.138 (notfleshka/android-kernel-samsung-e1s); device runs 6.1.162.
Derived from src/userns/user_namespace.c (6.12 port) and reconciled with the
vendor 6.1 file: no binfmt_misc member, kmemdup instead of kmemdup_array.
live-proc.c matches the vendor fs/proc/base.c map/setgroups handlers.
compat/linux/user_namespace.h is the vendor 6.1 header with init_user_ns
treated as immortal (get/put skip it); layout is the vendor's.

Stage 1 (USERNS61=1, DS_USERNS_PIN): user namespace creation via unshare,
uid/gid/projid maps and setgroups through /proc/<pid>, setns into a user
namespace, and getuid/geteuid/getgid/getegid mapped at output.

Unlike src/userns, the native credential lifecycle (prepare_creds,
commit_creds, __put_cred, put_cred_rcu, copy_creds) is NOT replaced: on
Samsung KDP it allocates and seals creds through the hypervisor and must stay
the vendor's. Because that native code was built without CONFIG_USER_NS, it
never takes or drops user_ns references; DS_USERNS_PIN therefore holds one
extra reference per namespace so none is freed while a cred may point to it.
Each namespace stays allocated until reboot (stage 2 replaces this with
reference tracking). clone(CLONE_NEWUSER) still fails with EINVAL through the
native copy_creds.

Not a security boundary yet: file permission, capability-over-inode, stat,
exec and other identity paths still use native checks (see src/userns
live-*.c for what the 6.12 port additionally restores).

Device validation (SM-S921N, S5E9945, 6.1.162-android14-11, Samsung EL2 kprobe
mode, vendor-header build f0ace0b7...): activate=1 exposes /proc/self/ns/user;
`droidspaces check` passes; inside a Droidspaces container with Allow
Sandboxing, `unshare -r id` as uid 1000 reports uid=0 gid=0 and
/proc/self/uid_map reads "0 1000 1"; PID and IPC namespaces still work from
the same module. Known gap: getgroups returns unmapped host gids (no
live-groups yet).

Stage 1.5 A (identity):
- live-identity.c: vendor kernel/sys.c __sys_set{,re,res,fs}{u,g}id and
  getres{u,g}id verbatim (DEFEX checks kept) with CONFIG_USER_NS, redirected
  at the __sys_* helpers (native and compat wrappers call them; GKI vmlinux
  confirms no inlining). The syscall wrappers are left alone because Samsung
  KernelSU kretprobes __arm64_sys_setresuid.
- live-groups.c: vendor kernel/groups.c getgroups/setgroups with mapped ids;
  may_setgroups is redirected and refuses compat tasks in a child namespace
  (setgroups16 still converts ids natively).
- live-capabilities.c: vendor security/commoncap.c cap_task_fix_setuid and
  cap_bprm_creds_from_file. The latter first undoes set-id bits that the
  inlined bprm_fill_uid applied for an owner or group unmapped in the new
  namespace (it was built with kuid_has_mapping() always true).
- userns_scope=1 (default): only tasks in a module PID namespace or with
  CAP_SYS_ADMIN may create user namespaces until stage 1.5 B lands.
Stage 1.5 A device validation (SM-S921N, a041e784...): in a container with
Allow Sandboxing, `unshare -r id` shows groups=0(root),65534(nobody);
/proc/self/status keeps Uid/Gid 1000 inside the namespace, after
`setpriv --reuid=0 --regid=0 --keep-groups`, and when running sudo (which
fails: uid 1 is unmapped); setgroups after setgroups=deny is refused as
upstream. Outside, `sudo id` in the container and KernelSU su still work.

Stage 1.5 B device validation (SM-S921N, a040781a...): `droidspaces check`
and KernelSU su unaffected. In a container with Allow Sandboxing, as uid
1000: stat shows the home directory as 0:0 and /etc/passwd as
65534:65534; /etc/shadow stays unreadable; `unshare -rm` mounts tmpfs and
new files show owner 0:0; `unshare -rmpf --mount-proc` sees its own pid 1;
`bwrap --unshare-all --ro-bind / / --proc /proc --dev /dev --tmpfs /tmp id`
runs (clone(CLONE_NEWUSER) through the vendor copy_creds), and
/proc/self/status inside keeps Uid/Gid 1000.

Rootless Podman device validation (SM-S921N, build-id ...433df73b), as uid
1000 with Allow Sandboxing, `--storage-driver=vfs --runtime crun
--cgroups=disabled --security-opt no-new-privileges`: alpine runs as
mapped root (uid_map 0 1000 1 / 1 100000 65536), /dev/mqueue mounts
(internal mqueue super_block owned in create_ipc_ns), and --network=pasta
gives DNS and HTTP. Raw ICMP works with --cap-add NET_RAW.

Known gaps, to fix:
- seccomp filters need no_new_privs: seccomp_prepare_filter() checks
  CAP_SYS_ADMIN against the native current_user_ns() (init_user_ns), so a
  container root without no_new_privs gets EACCES. The 6.12 port
  (src/userns) has the same limitation (docs/docker-plz110-v0.1.4.md).
- net.ipv4.ping_group_range: the native sysctl handler converts the range
  with init_user_ns, so Podman's default "0 0" written from the container
  means host gid 0 and unprivileged ICMP sockets fail for container root
  (gid 1000 on the host). "0 2147483647" works. Needs the handler to map
  through the writer's user namespace, as upstream.
- FUSE mounts from a child user namespace (fuse-overlayfs) not yet ported.
